/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  convDryer03_sugar_crossflow  --  the CROSS-FLOW dryer (belt / plug-flow
                                   fluid bed).

  THE THREE ARRANGEMENTS ON ONE FEED AND ONE AIR.  convDryer01 sends half of
  the feed through a co-current and half through a counter-current dryer;
  this case is the third half-feed (1711.5 kg/h dry sucrose at X_in = 0.20
  kg/kg, 330 K; 400 kmol/h of air at 420 K, 2 mol% water), the same dryer
  data (A = 300 m2, k_Y = 0.05 kg/(m2 s), X_c = 0.12, f = Phi^1.5), so the
  three outlets compare one to one:

                      co-current   counter-current   cross-flow
    X out [kg/kg]       0.02359       0.00474          0.00902
    Y out [kg/kg dry]   0.03930       0.04214          0.04149
    T air out [K]       359.7         353.3            354.7
    T solid out [K]     316.1         315.7            315.7
    X_c crossed at      6.4 % of A    12.4 % of A      27.2 % of A

  Theory: Theory Guide sec:dryer-flow-arrangements; gate
  check_dryer_arrangements (an independent integration of each arrangement).

  The solid travels in plug flow along the length; EVERY length element is
  fed with the INLET air, which leaves it once, and the exhaust is the mix of
  the elements (Mujumdar 2006 s.3.6.2.3.1, Eq. 3.66-3.78, Figs. 3.8-3.9).
  The air never depletes along the length: each element's rate is set by
  fresh air at 420 K, so the first quarter of the length dries at a CONSTANT
  rate (the solid sits at the inlet air's wet bulb, 315.7 K; the air that
  leaves those elements is nearly saturated at 319.7 K) and the falling-rate
  period then runs against the same fresh-air driving force.  The local air
  leaving the last element is the hottest and driest (415.6 K), the first the
  coolest; the exhaust is their mix.

  Driving force: operation.drivingForce logMean (default, Eq. 3.73: the air
  plugs through the element's area) or inlet (Eq. 3.72, valid for small
  k_Y A / G; refused by name where an element would leave above Y*).
  Refused by name: air recirculation, axial dispersion (Eq. 3.79-3.84), a
  two-dimensional bed, a non-uniform air distribution, too little air.
  HYPOTHESES (announced): the solid at the inlet air's wet bulb all along;
  Lewis = 1; one k_Y; uniform air (Eq. 3.76); adiabatic; the solid's step from
  330 K to that wet bulb is spread over the elements in proportion to their air.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

// stream state lives on disk in 0/ (per-stream files); flowsheetDict is TOPOLOGY only

units
(
    {
        name        dryer;
        type        convectiveDryer;
        inputs      ( wetSolid  hotAir );
        outputs     ( drySolid  exhaust );
        operation
        {
            flow              crossflow;
            area              300 m2;        // gas-solid contact area
            k_Y               [1 -2 -1 0 0]  0.05;
            criticalMoisture  0.12;           // kg/kg dry solid
            dryingCurve
            {
                shape     power;
                exponent  1.5;
                source    "hypothetical teaching curve -- not a measured material";
            }
        }
    }
);
